// mgeHost64 — W23: the caustic map's SHAPE, shared by the generator and the consumer.
//
// Constants only, deliberately: caustic.srt.h declares the compute SRT and a colour frag must not
// pull that in (one SRT per header, [[project_forge_srt_one_per_header]]), but both sides have to
// agree on how many depth slices the array has or the consumer's slice index runs off the end. So
// the two numbers that BOTH sides need live here and nothing else does.
//
// Mirrored host-side by kCausticRes / kCausticSlices in forgerender.cpp. Nothing can static_assert
// across the FSL boundary, so the host logs the pair at creation and that log line is the check.
#pragma once

// 512 and not 256. At 256 over a 512-unit tile one texel is 2 units — and a texel 2 units across,
// on a seabed 100 units away through a 935-pixel focal length, covers ~19 SCREEN pixels. That is
// what "a bit low res" was: the map, not the pattern. Cost goes as RES^2 (the beam footprint is
// scale-invariant in texels, so cells-per-splat does not move and only the splat COUNT does), which
// is paid for by updating a subset of the depth slices each frame — see kCausticSlicesPerFrame.
#ifndef CAUSTIC_RES
#define CAUSTIC_RES    512
#endif
// ⚠ W26: FIVE, AND THE FIFTH IS ABOUT REACH, NOT DETAIL. The consumer fades the deviation out one
// slice past the last one, so the ladder's top entry IS where caustics stop — and with the W23
// ladder that was 806 units (11.5 m), where the downwelling coefficient still delivers 49% of the
// surface's blue light to the seabed. Caustics ending in the middle of plainly lit water is a hard
// edge nothing in the physics asked for. The host ladder now runs 32..1250, so the fade-out lands
// at 3125 units (45 m) where blue is down to 6%.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS A FIFTH SLICE RATHER THAN A WIDER FOUR. Reaching 45 m on four slices needs 1.66-octave
// spacing, and the deepest of those would sit at ~1000 units where the generator's own sun-disc blur
// is still under a texel — i.e. a near-sharp pattern held out to 45 m, which is the "the pattern
// stops evolving" failure the consumer's FADE-DO-NOT-CLAMP note already warns about wearing a
// different hat. A real slice at 1250 is generated WITH its correct 1.1-texel softening.
//
// ⚠ COST IS NOT UNCHANGED, WHICH IS WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO CLAIM. The dispatch is: n slices per
// frame whatever the ladder's length, so only the round-robin PERIOD moves (2 -> 2.5 frames, ~1.25
// texels of lag on the fastest component). But a DEEPER slice costs more to splat — the beam lands
// c*D*grad(h) from its own cell, ~39 destination texels RMS at 1250 units against ~11 at 360, so
// neighbouring threads scatter to unrelated cache lines and the accumulator's atomics stop
// coalescing. The 13x13 kernel cap bounds the CELL COUNT and bounds nothing else. Measured with the
// whole ladder rebuilt in one frame: 1.09 ms for 32/72/160/360, 3.14 ms for 32/80/200/500/1250, so
// the shipping 2 slices/frame went 0.55 -> 1.26 ms. See kCausticDepths in forgerender.cpp for the
// numbers, why `caustic=` in the heartbeat cannot see any of it, and where to buy it back.
// The other price is one more 512² plane in the map and one in the accumulator.
#ifndef CAUSTIC_SLICES
#define CAUSTIC_SLICES 5
#endif

// ─── W24: THE DYNAMIC LAYERS' SLICES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The same 512² array carries three layers, and they share one texture for one reason: eight slices
// is one SRV, one UAV<->SRV barrier and one bindCausticField into all seven PerFrame sets, where
// three textures would be three of each. Nothing else about them is shared — different source
// grids, different world domains, different clear values.
//
// ⚠ THE STATIC SLICES MUST STAY AT 0..CAUSTIC_SLICES-1. The static generator round-robins its
// cursor modulo CAUSTIC_SLICES and the consumer's log-depth index is pinned to that range, so the
// dynamic layers are APPENDED above them and every static index is untouched. That is what makes
// "dynamic strength 0 is pixel-identical to the pre-W24 build" a thing that can be checked.
//
// ⚠ AND THE ACCUMULATOR PLANES FOLLOW THE SAME INDEX. accum index = (slice*RES + y)*RES + x, so
// growing the buffer from 5 planes to 8 cannot move a static entry. Only the tail is new.
//
// ⚠ W26 MOVED THE DYNAMIC BASE, AND THAT IS THE COST OF INSERTING A STATIC SLICE — the invariant
// above is "the static slices own 0..CAUSTIC_SLICES-1", not "the dynamic ones never move", so a
// fifth static slice pushes ripple to 5..6 and wake to 7. Nothing has to be migrated: every plane
// is rebuilt or re-primed by its own group's resolve before anything reads it, and the host's
// static_asserts are written relatively so they keep checking the layout rather than the numbers.
//
// RIPPLE gets TWO depths because the near field is where the depth evolution is visible and where
// the camera actually is (first person, swimming). WAKE gets ONE: wake wavelengths are 90-200 units
// and do not fold until ~700-950 units of depth, so the whole playable range is PRE-FOCUS, where
// the deviation is linear in depth and one reference slice scaled by depth is exact to first order.
#ifndef CAUSTIC_RIPPLE_BASE
#define CAUSTIC_RIPPLE_BASE   5
#endif
#ifndef CAUSTIC_RIPPLE_SLICES
#define CAUSTIC_RIPPLE_SLICES 2
#endif
#ifndef CAUSTIC_WAKE_SLICE
#define CAUSTIC_WAKE_SLICE    7
#endif
#ifndef CAUSTIC_TOTAL_SLICES
#define CAUSTIC_TOTAL_SLICES  8
#endif

// The WAKE layer's single reference depth, in world units. Shared with the consumer, which scales
// the deviation by depth/this — the reference cancels out of that ratio to first order, which is
// exactly why one slice suffices and why this is a constant rather than a slider.
#ifndef CAUSTIC_WAKE_DEPTH
#define CAUSTIC_WAKE_DEPTH 256.0f
#endif

// ─── W26: HOW DEEP EACH DYNAMIC LAYER IS ALLOWED TO REACH, IN WORLD UNITS ───────────────────────
//
// ⚠ THESE ARE THE MODEL'S VALIDITY, NOT A LADDER'S END, and that is why they are world units rather
// than the slice-index arithmetic they replace. The wake's deviation is scaled by depth on the
// first-order argument that wake wavelengths (90-200 units) do not fold until ~700-950 units of
// water — past ~5 m that stops being "linear in depth" and becomes an EXTRAPOLATION of a pattern
// stored at 256 units, which is exactly what "looks aliased after 5 m" was reporting. A puddle
// splash is smaller still: a ripple is 10-22 units of wavelength born by an actor's foot, and its
// caustic has no business being legible a metre and a half down.
//
// 1 unit = 1.43 cm, so 350 = 5 m and 70 = 1 m. Each layer fades to nothing over the NEXT HALF of
// its window (7.5 m / 1.5 m), so the window is ONE number and the fade follows it — see the consumer
// in waterfog.h.fsl. Both are the DEFAULTS of host knobs (causticWakeMaxDepth /
// causticRippleMaxDepth), which read these macros directly rather than mirroring them.
//
// ⚠ WATER SHALLOWER THAN THE WINDOW IS BIT-IDENTICAL. The wake's old `clamp(depth/256, 0, 4)` only
// binds past 1024 units, and at 350 the expression already reads 1.367 — so this deletes the
// 350->1024 growth region and nothing else. That region is precisely the range that was reported.
#ifndef CAUSTIC_WAKE_MAXDEPTH
#define CAUSTIC_WAKE_MAXDEPTH   350.0f
#endif
#ifndef CAUSTIC_RIPPLE_MAXDEPTH
#define CAUSTIC_RIPPLE_MAXDEPTH 70.0f
#endif
